WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:09.500
- Hi everybody.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.950
Adam Metallo with the
Digitization Program Office,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.280
at the Smithsonian.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.040
It's under the office of the
Chief Information Officer there

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.450
and we work with all
the museums, archives,

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.520
libraries, galleries to increase
the quality and quantity

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.250
of digitization across the organization.

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:28.510
Specifically, my role is
to increase the impact

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:29.680
of said digitization.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.040
So how do we get the maximum
mileage out of all the work

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.050
that we're putting into what we're doing,

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.600
and computer vision and machine learning

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.940
and things like this are one
of the most exciting ways

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.030
that I think that we
can show the potential

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:42.820
of our collection at scale,

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:43.740
and so this is definitely something

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.790
that merits a lot of attention and focus.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.440
This is sort of two parts.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.030
First I've been haunting
a lot of trade show,

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.170
industry conferences
over the past few months,

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:55.520
to see what other industries

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:57.440
are doing with the technology,

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.440
how they're thinking about it,

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.343
how far along one group is versus another.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:06.510
So the Nvidia conference late last year,

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.480
they've convinced me that GPUs will solve

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:09.913
all of our problems,

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:12.410
but beyond the propaganda,

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:14.010
it's clear that this is having an effect

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:15.280
across all industries.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.490
This is not something that's unique,

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:19.200
and we'll definitely
need to make sense of it

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.300
sooner or later,

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.253
and sooner's ideal in my mind.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.600
I went to a conference run by AAM,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.200
called Museums and New Intelligences.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.960
The emphasis there was really
on in gallery experiences,

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.180
so imagine maybe a camera
tracking visitors' expressions,

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.990
and they may be happy or
sad or bored or excited,

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.510
and that's kind of a way to get a metric.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:43.920
I think that's super interesting.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.060
I also think that's something

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.020
that is going to be commoditized

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:49.780
by a lot of other industries,

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.250
and that's probably not the domain

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.243
that we need to worry too
much about making progress.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:02:00.800
GSA Future Services Now was a
government centric conference.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.470
The clear message there
was that across agencies,

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:05.060
they're thinking about this technology,

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:06.980
and they're aggregating there as well,

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.640
and so they're one major
set of stakeholders

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.510
that the Smithsonian and
anything cultural heritage

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:15.203
can look to to leverage our
data against government data.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.060
National Academy of Sciences -
The Science of Deep Learning.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.690
A lot of the scientists went
out of their way to say,

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:25.160
this is not a black box.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.880
It's a very big complicated box,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.860
and you don't know how it
works until you've built it,

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.580
and you've run it, and see how it breaks.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.710
But there is recourse
when things go wrong,

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:40.430
and you can go back and see
how you need to tweak things,

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:41.900
and I think the real trick with this stuff

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.560
is the lack of transparency
that most industries have

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.870
in making models available,

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:49.300
and for us, I think one of our superpowers

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.380
that we can leverage here, is our ability

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.130
to be more transparent with these models,

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:57.400
and we could play a major role with ethics

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.440
and standard operating procedures

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.453
for how we use this technology.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:08.820
Fantastic Futures was a conference put on

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.540
with Stanford University Libraries

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.433
and National Library of Norway,

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:15.717
and there, librarians are great.

00:03:15.717 --> 00:03:18.007
They can adapt to anything and say,

00:03:18.007 --> 00:03:20.420
"Yeah, that's stuff librarians do."

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.527
And they were pretty ready to say,

00:03:22.527 --> 00:03:25.290
"Yeah, it really doesn't
make sense to strictly think

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:26.940
in terms of the libraries.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.490
This is a scenario where

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.980
we're all stronger together
across disciplines."

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.810
Another really critical thing there was,

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.570
I heard a bit about
triangulating low quality,

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:42.520
low accuracy data to get
high quality of results.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.560
And if you think about
our collections records,

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.630
when we get our collections
records perfect,

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:48.660
we put them out into the world,

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.070
and we're very proud of it.

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:52.010
When our collections
records are not perfect,

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:56.180
we hide them in shame
until we either retire,

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.510
or until we have time to get
around to making it perfect,

00:03:58.510 --> 00:03:59.700
then we'll release it,

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:03.110
and I think that finding a way

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.960
to make this imperfect data visible

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:07.900
and something that we can compute against

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:12.470
to bootstrap the quality of our data

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.283
is a critical possibility.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.730
It's a standard explainer

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:22.210
for machine learning versus
classical programming,

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:25.090
so this was useful for
me to wrap my head around

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:26.540
a really fundamental difference

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:27.610
between what we're doing here,

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:30.290
and I think this is the cultural change

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.260
that a lot of folks across the Smithsonian

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:35.143
are gonna need to wrap
their heads around as well.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:38.593
You sort of switch the
equation a little bit.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:42.580
I think the really interesting thing

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.510
is the possibility of bootstrapping,

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:47.720
and the fact that we do
have all the raw ingredients

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.570
with our data as it stands, flaws and all,

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:52.560
to get much better data

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.393
out of what we currently
have at our disposal.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:03.110
Moving on to a workshop
and presentation series

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:04.610
that we did in a collaboration

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.163
with Google Arts and Culture,

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:10.513
and the Data Science
Lab with Rebecca Dikow.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:14.690
Basically the goal here
was to introduce people

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:15.760
from across the Smithsonian

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.070
to general premises of machine learning

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:20.460
through a webcast and lecture series,

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:21.890
and then we also did a workshop

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:24.600
where we invited a bunch of people

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.470
from different disciplines
from across the Smithsonian

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:30.860
to think about how this
applies to their own work.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:33.100
You can see the very credibly gentleman

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:33.970
on the left hand side there

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:37.087
with his hand gestures speaking (mumbles).

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:41.250
So very simply, the goals here

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:45.100
were to provide a basic
introduction to machine learning,

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:48.890
try to identify what our
common challenges are,

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:50.470
and then also to be very deliberate

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:53.680
about developing grounds
for collaboration,

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.790
very much like what
we're doing here today.

00:05:56.790 --> 00:06:00.530
And I think what keeps me
excited about this stuff

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:01.860
is just this visualization,

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.960
and defining what this long tail

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.060
of our cultural heritage collections are,

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.850
if we can do that, and the more
specifically we can do that,

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.110
I think the more important we become,

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:14.200
not just at getting good
at using these tools,

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:16.950
but at giving back to the industry,

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:19.860
and playing a role in how
AI and machine learning

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:23.020
and machine vision and every
variation of these terms

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:24.173
is affecting our world.

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:27.500
Tried to visualize this a
handful of different ways,

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.240
because nobody ever seems to think

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.380
that's as important as I do.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:33.340
This is something I pulled
together on the train,

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:34.293
it's kind of lame.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.150
I think this is more
compelling, but not as accurate.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.250
So how did we go about
organizing this event?

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.860
We had approximately 50
different participants,

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:49.060
so on the left hand side here,

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:51.850
you can see representation
from different units.

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:53.570
Sometimes we had a couple
of people from a unit,

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:54.953
sometimes just one rep,

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:59.770
and basically we organized
them into different cohorts

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:00.603
with different themes.

00:07:00.603 --> 00:07:02.890
Accessibility, American
Women's History Initiative,

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:07.260
Art, History, Culture,
Informatics and Metadata, Science,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:10.530
Search and Discovery,
Transcription and Translation.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:12.600
And as you can see by this next slide,

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.120
ooh, nope, I'll wait for that punchline.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.810
We came up with about
65 independent concepts

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:19.643
that were derived,

00:07:19.643 --> 00:07:21.227
and some of these were
one-offs, somebody said,

00:07:21.227 --> 00:07:22.500
"Hey, this would be a great idea,"

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:23.910
write it down, that's a concept.

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.100
Other things that were brought
to the table were things

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.370
that folks had been thinking
about for months or years,

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:31.058
and wanna bring to the table

00:07:31.058 --> 00:07:34.453
as a possible project to move forward.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:37.163
And now you can see,

00:07:37.163 --> 00:07:39.380
as we break down all
these individual concepts,

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:43.060
and try to find what the
relationship is between them,

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:44.490
we now have a tangled mess.

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:47.420
When we tried to thematically organize

00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:48.773
what our interests were.

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.480
It no longer made sense,

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:55.420
and so the physical boundaries
of the walls of our museums,

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:57.810
as we make the digital transition,

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.650
are making less and less sense,

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:02.810
but I think that with this
wave of new technology,

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:06.460
they are going to make less
sense far more quickly,

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:08.633
and it's something that
we can prepare for.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:12.111
Some of the critical strategic interests

00:08:12.111 --> 00:08:14.550
were bias of course,

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:17.580
and understanding the bias
in our own collections.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:21.080
This is something I feel
strongly is a risk, of course,

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:25.663
but it's a potential benefit
as much as it is a liability,

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:30.490
so I'll try to talk a
little bit more about that.

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:33.150
Data and training,
collections enhancement,

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:36.629
so that would be the idea of
bootstrapping our metadata

00:08:36.629 --> 00:08:39.210
up into something higher
quality, higher accuracy.

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:40.360
Audience driven priorities,

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:41.793
things like enhanced search,

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:45.313
like we've seen examples of today as well.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:51.290
We also can organize these ideas

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:53.680
into more tech-centric themes,

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.830
and again, it's a tangled mess of knots

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:59.200
where our traditional disciplines

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.113
no longer make as much sense,

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.760
and it also means a
tremendous opportunity for us

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:09.760
to be more efficient about how we develop.

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:12.740
So I'm gonna zoom in here to, specifically

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:14.580
the American Women's History Initiative

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:16.030
concepts that were developed,

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:20.500
and what we're looking at
here on the left hand side,

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:22.540
is the group of about four people

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:25.730
who are working on the American
Women's History cohort,

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:28.000
and if you track the purple lines on top

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.580
over to the right hand side,

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:30.470
those were the ideas
that they came up with.

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:33.200
Don't worry about reading
the fine print too much.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:38.200
Down here we have other
cohorts that came up with ideas

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:40.660
that were strongly
related to or applicable

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:42.320
to American Women's History,

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:45.513
and so you can see those popping
up on the right hand side.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:49.100
And then basically, what you have here

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:51.980
is a list of ideas that do the most good

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:53.640
for the most people

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:57.020
based on the interests
expressed at this workshop,

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:59.580
as opposed to what might be the project

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:00.660
represented on top,

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:03.630
which may have all the virtue
in the world in and of itself,

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:07.233
but does not offer as much
opportunity for us to build upon.

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:10.970
We're gonna track backwards now,

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:13.453
and see how else this
method might be useful.

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:18.940
At this layer we can see all
the key internal stakeholders,

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:22.080
so what units across the
Smithsonian are interested

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:23.563
in these particular concepts.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:26.500
What was that individual origin concept

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:31.500
that got truncated into these
more overarching themes,

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:33.030
and essentially we have all the details

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:35.900
that are so difficult in getting traction

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:36.973
on a given project.

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:43.160
So this isn't an argument
to think beyond our grasp,

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:46.540
but it is an argument to
initiate and design projects

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:48.870
with lots of open doors for expansion,

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:51.790
and new opportunities when
we come to a conclusion

00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:53.400
on these collaborations,

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:54.550
and I think that's what

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:56.783
this type of mindset might help with.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:00.909
And I think the point here is that,

00:11:00.909 --> 00:11:04.270
we can overlay any type of
content with this concept.

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:06.450
So these were a number
of different proposals

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:09.350
that stemmed, some from the workshop,

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:11.690
and some were ideas that
were in progress originally,

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:16.520
and that were proposed as
collaborations with Google,

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:17.460
but the exciting thing is that

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:19.430
we could take any other idea

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:21.030
and basically do an analysis

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:25.430
of how effective it would be
for our overarching goals,

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:27.950
and we can be pretty flexible

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:29.370
in terms of which content

00:11:29.370 --> 00:11:31.363
we choose to run with in this respect.

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:38.390
And in terms of bias, and I
think the shackles example,

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:40.870
which is really important,

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:43.950
on one hand it's a huge liability

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:46.390
when we mislabel an object like that,

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:48.050
and it can be hugely damaging.

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:49.150
I don't want to underestimate

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:51.360
the severity of that as an issue,

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:53.690
but I also think that there is a flip side

00:11:53.690 --> 00:11:56.030
and an opportunity there,

00:11:56.030 --> 00:11:57.130
and in the end what happened

00:11:57.130 --> 00:12:00.170
is that surfaced a deficiency

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:01.300
in the model that's being used,

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:04.070
and I think that's a really
critical role that we can play

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:06.180
and cultural heritage in general

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:07.910
with our data on the periphery

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:11.660
of what general tech
companies have access to,

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:14.260
we're really gonna be
the critical last mile

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:18.260
in making a lot of what
they wanna do possible.

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:23.260
So it's basically, I'm advocating for us

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:26.640
to value what we have to
offer the rest of industry,

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:27.620
as much as we're excited

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:30.223
about how we can use this tech in house.

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:32.665
And that's all I'll say.

00:12:32.665 --> 00:12:33.498
- [Announcer] Thank you so much.

00:12:33.498 --> 00:12:34.331
- Thanks.

00:12:34.331 --> 00:12:37.331
(audience clapping)

00:12:39.029 --> 00:12:41.612
(serene music)

